# Bedrock-Patch: 0003-bedrock-network-blocking-hook
# Area: integration
# Upstream-Paths: services/network/url_loader_factory.cc
# Reason: phase 7 — the one call site that makes the blocking pipeline real. Every
#   subresource request enters URLLoaderFactory::CreateLoaderAndStartWithSyncClient,
#   which is before a URLLoader exists, so a blocked request needs no unwinding:
#   the client is completed with net::ERR_BLOCKED_BY_CLIENT exactly the way the
#   existing keepalive-exhaustion path does it.
#   The patch carries no policy. It maps RequestDestination to Bedrock's own
#   RequestKind (explicitly, so a new upstream value is a compile error rather
#   than a silent "other"), takes the registrable domain from Chromium's
#   public-suffix list, reads the top-level document from the request's or the
#   factory's IsolationInfo, and asks bedrock::integration::DecideRequest. The
#   verdict, the built-in list and the reason strings all live in
#   src_overrides/bedrock, host-tested by network_hook_test.
#   It logs one [bedrock] line per block naming the rule, and one line at first
#   use stating how many rules are loaded and that profile settings are not
#   wired in yet — see build/ENFORCEMENT.md, build 5.
# Owner: integration
# Chromium-Version: 151.0.7922.173
# Upstream-Bug: none
# Drop-When: the profile's protection settings reach the network service (then the
#   engine is per-NetworkContext, not process-wide), or upstream grows a
#   supported request-blocking extension point we can use instead.
diff --git a/services/network/url_loader_factory.cc b/services/network/url_loader_factory.cc
index ef93f794f3..ed38da42e3 100644
--- a/services/network/url_loader_factory.cc
